<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Golden Gate (Kyiv)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1037</span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> · Also known as Golden Gate of Kyiv, Golden Gate of Kiev</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Golden Gate was the main ceremonial entrance of medieval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyiv">Kyiv</a>, built under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yaroslav-the-wise">Yaroslav the Wise</a> around 1037 as part of the city's new fortifications, with a church above its passage. It was badly damaged when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> sacked Kyiv in 1240 and later fell into ruin. The surviving remains were enclosed in a reconstruction in 1982. The Golden Gate of Vladimir was modelled on it.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/golden-gate-kyiv.jpg" alt="Golden Gate (Kyiv)" width="320" height="226" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: George Chernilevsky · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Golden_Gate_Kiev_2018_G1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Golden Gate was the main gate in the 11th-century fortifications of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyiv">Kyiv</a>, the capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kievan-rus">Kievan Rus'</a>, and one of three built by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yaroslav-the-wise">Yaroslav the Wise</a>, at about the time of the Saint Sophia Cathedral. It was first called the Southern Gate, one of three main entrances to the walled city beside the Ladski and Zhydivski gates, which have not survived. The stone fortifications ran for only 3.5 kilometres. The name was probably given in imitation of the Golden Gate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>.</p><p>Once the Church of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/annunciation">Annunciation</a>, or Blahovist Church, was built beside it, its golden domes were a landmark seen from outside the city, and the gate took the name Golden. Its passage was about 12 metres high and 6 metres wide.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>For almost five hundred years the gate served as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyiv">Kyiv</a>'s triumphal arch and a symbol of the city. A church over the passage, mentioned in the chronicle and in Metropolitan Ilarion's Word of Law and Grace, was meant to give the city heavenly protection and was also a place of worship. In 1240 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/batu-khan">Batu Khan</a>'s Golden Horde partly destroyed the gate, which remained in use for ceremonies into the 18th century while gradually falling into ruin. A gate in Vladimir, where Andrei I Bogolyubsky founded his own state, was later given a similar name. In 1832 Metropolitan Eugenius had the ruins excavated and surveyed, and works in the 1970s added a pavilion housing a museum.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Only two wall segments of the original, 24 and 13 metres long, of Roman brick and stone, survive. No images of the original gate survive either. The Soviet authorities rebuilt it completely in 1982, for the 1500th anniversary of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyiv">Kyiv</a>, a decision that has been intensely controversial because many competing reconstructions had been proposed.</p><p>Some art historians called for the reconstruction to be demolished so that the ruins could be seen. The medieval sections are protected as a monument of national significance, while the reconstructed outer parts are not listed. Inside is a branch of the museum Sophia of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyiv">Kyiv</a>, and a monument to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yaroslav-the-wise">Yaroslav the Wise</a> stands beside it since 1997.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Golden Gate, Kyiv” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
